WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.280
Which four chromosomes does a fruit fly sperm get?

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.220
The fly's body cells hold eight.

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.820
Its eggs and sperm hold four, one from each of four pairs.

4
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:18.130
Mendel worked out his laws from counts of peas.

5
00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.230
He never saw what did the separating.

6
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:26.613
These two chromosomes are homologs, one inherited from each parent.

7
00:00:27.873 --> 00:00:30.153
They carry the same genes at the same places.

8
00:00:31.413 --> 00:00:34.133
But they can carry different versions, called alleles.

9
00:00:35.473 --> 00:00:38.233
Blue carries one allele, and orange carries the other.

10
00:00:39.573 --> 00:00:40.573
Follow the colours.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:45.043
Before meiosis, in S phase, each chromosome is copied.

12
00:00:46.343 --> 00:00:49.923
The two copies are sister chromatids, joined at the centromere.

13
00:00:51.303 --> 00:00:52.843
Count centromeres, not arms.

14
00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:56.463
A copied chromosome is still one chromosome.

15
00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:01.053
Meiosis is one round of copying, then two divisions.

16
00:01:02.413 --> 00:01:06.393
In the first, the homologs find each other and line up side by side.

17
00:01:07.673 --> 00:01:12.113
Then the pair sits across the middle of the cell, its homologs facing opposite

18
00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:13.113
poles.

19
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:16.327
Anaphase I pulls the homologs apart.

20
00:01:17.647 --> 00:01:19.847
Each new cell gets one member of the pair.

21
00:01:21.087 --> 00:01:22.747
Count its centromeres: one.

22
00:01:23.847 --> 00:01:27.447
The cell is already haploid, with one homolog of this pair.

23
00:01:28.647 --> 00:01:31.267
That chromosome is still two sister chromatids.

24
00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.307
No DNA is copied in between.

25
00:01:35.627 --> 00:01:37.767
In the second division, the sisters part.

26
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:40.227
That makes four products.

27
00:01:41.427 --> 00:01:44.007
Two carry blue's allele, and two carry orange's.

28
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.590
That two-and-two split is what Mendel's model expects.

29
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.350
It is his law of segregation, seen as chromosome movement.

30
00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:59.650
Now add a second pair, green and purple, on a different chromosome, carrying a

31
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:00.650
second gene.

32
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:02.663
Here is the question.

33
00:02:04.043 --> 00:02:08.203
Does the way the blue pair faces the poles change the way the green pair faces?

34
00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:19.687
No. Each pair faces the spindle by its own coin toss.

35
00:02:20.987 --> 00:02:23.487
Blue may end up with green, or blue with purple.

36
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.900
Ignoring crossovers for now, one meiosis takes just one of these layouts.

37
00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.720
So one meiosis gives only two kinds of gamete, two of each.

38
00:02:36.083 --> 00:02:39.143
Independence shows only when you count many meioses.

39
00:02:40.583 --> 00:02:42.223
Both layouts are equally likely.

40
00:02:43.503 --> 00:02:46.283
So all four kinds of gamete appear, a quarter each.

41
00:02:47.523 --> 00:02:49.143
That is independent assortment.

42
00:02:50.283 --> 00:02:52.763
Each extra pair is one more coin toss.

43
00:02:53.943 --> 00:02:57.803
So the combinations climb fast, with two choices for every pair.

44
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:01.327
This holds for genes on different chromosomes.

45
00:03:02.907 --> 00:03:06.687
Genes close together on one chromosome tend to travel together.

46
00:03:07.907 --> 00:03:11.127
And something has to hold each pair together until it is dealt.

47
00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:13.527
That is chapter 2.

48
00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:18.047
So here is the idea, in the simple Mendelian model.

49
00:03:19.507 --> 00:03:22.547
Meiosis I deals one homolog of each pair to each cell.

50
00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:26.087
Each pair is dealt by its own fair coin toss.

51
00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:29.267
Meiosis II then splits sisters.

52
00:03:30.807 --> 00:03:32.067
Segregation is the deal.

53
00:03:33.807 --> 00:03:35.867
Independent assortment is the coin tosses.
